  "generated_text": "1. To convert the point \\((-2, -2)\\) from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates, we use the following formulas:\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate \\(r\\):\n   \\[\n   r = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-2)^2} = \\sqrt{4 + 4} = \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\(\\theta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{-2}{-2}\\right) = \\tan^{-1}(1)\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\tan^{-1}(1) = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\):\n   \\[\n   \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of the point \\((-2, -2)\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{(2\\sqrt{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{4})}\n\\]\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[(2*sqrt(2), pi/4), '(2\\\\sqrt{2}, \\\\frac{\\\\pi}{4})']",
    "gold_answer": "We have that $r = \\sqrt{(-2)^2 + (-2)^2} = 2 \\sqrt{2}.$  Also, if we draw the line connecting the origin and $(-2,2),$ this line makes an angle of $\\frac{5 \\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.8 cm);\n\ndraw((-3.5,0)--(3.5,0));\ndraw((0,-3.5)--(0,3.5));\ndraw(arc((0,0),2*sqrt(2),0,225),red,Arrow(6));\ndraw((0,0)--(-2,-2));\n\ndot((-2,-2), red);\nlabel(\"$(-2,-2)$\", (-2,-2), SE, UnFill);\ndot((2*sqrt(2),0), red);\n[/asy]\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates are $\\boxed{\\left( 2 \\sqrt{2}, \\frac{5 \\pi}{4} \\right)}.$",
    "is_correct": false
  },
